"reference request - What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit? - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/420897/what-is-the-smallest-size-of-a-shape-in-which-all-fixed-n-polyominos-can-fit)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 381\nL32: Let $n$ be an integer and consider all fixed $n$-polyominos, i.e., without rotation or reflection. I am interested in finding a shape in which all polyominos can embed. (It is OK if multiple polyominos overlap.)\nL33: \nL34: For instance, for $n=3$, the fixed 3-polyominos are:\nL35: \nL36:     `###  #..  ##.  ##.  #..  .#.\nL37:     ...  #..  #..  .#.  ##.  ##.\nL38:     ...  #..  ...  ...  ...  ...\nL39:     `\nL40: \nL41: and these polyominos all embed in the following shape with 5 cells, which is the best possible:\nL42: \nL43:     `.#.\nL44:     ###\nL45:     .#.\nL46:     `\nL47: More generally, a suitable shape for arbitrary $n$ is the $n \\times n$ square (with $n^2$ cells) and a naive lower bound would be $2n-1$ cells (necessary to embed the horizontal and vertical line $n$-polyomino).\nL48: \nL49: I define an integer sequence $S_n$ to be the minimal number of cells of a shape in which all $n$-polyominos embed, and I am interested in understanding this sequence. In particular, specific questions are:\nL50:   * Can we always find an optimal shape that fits into an $n \\times n$ square? (this seems intuitively reasonable but I do not know how to prove it)\nL51:   * Can we prove that, asymptotically, $S_n = \\Theta(n^2)$? (the challenge is to show an $\\Omega(n^2)$ lower bound -- maybe this is already possible by simply looking at a subset of the polyominos, but I couldn't see how to do it)\nL52: \nL53: More generally, has this sequence already been studied?\nL54: To understand what happens here, I was able to compute by bruteforce computer search the first values of $S_n$, making the assumption that optimal shapes always fit in an $n$ by $n$ square (first point above) -- these values may turn out not to be optimal if this assumption is wrong:\nL55: \nL56:   * We have $S_1 = 1$, $S_2 = 3$ (easily), and $S_3 = 5$ (see above)\nL57:   * We have $S_4=9$ with a surprising shape:\nL58: \nL59:     `..#.\nL60:     .##.\nL61:     ####\nL62:     .##.\nL63:     `\nL64:   * We have $S_5 = 13$, with the unsurprising shape:\nL65: \nL66:     `..#..\nL67:     .###.\nL68:     #####\nL69:     .###.\nL70:     ..#..\nL71:     `\nL72: \nL73:   * We have $S_6 = 18$ with a surprising shape:\nL74: \nL75:     `..##..\nL76:     ..##..\nL77:     ######\nL78:     #####.\nL79:     ..##..\nL80:     ..#...\nL81:     `\nL82: \nL83:   * We have $S_7 = 24$, the shape is similar to $n=5$ but with a hole:\nL84: \nL85:     `...#...\nL86:     ..###..\nL87:     .#.###.\nL88:     #######\nL89:     .#####.\nL90:     ..###..\nL91:     ...#...\nL92:     `\nL93: \nL94:   * I do not know $S_8$\nL95: There are matching sequences for these terms in OEIS, but their definitions do not seem relevant... Edit: maybe 【13†https://oeis.org/A203567†oeis.org】 【14†https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X01003570†www.sciencedirect.com】 would be worth investigating.\nL96: \nL97: Acknowledgement: This question is by 【15†Thomas Colcombet†www.irif.fr】 and 【16†Antonio Casares†www.labri.fr】.\nL98: \nL99: Edit: fixed the values of $S_5$ and $S_7$, many thanks to @RobPratt for noticing and reporting the errors!\nL100:   * 【17†reference-request】\nL101:   * 【18†co.combinatorics】\nL102:   * 【19†combinatorial-optimization】\nL103:   * 【20†integer-sequences】\nL104:   * 【21†extremal-combinatorics】\nL105: \nL106: [Button: Cite]\nL107: \nL108: 【22†Improve this question】\nL109: \nL110: Follow [Input]\nL111: \nL112: 【23†edited May 5, 2022 at 16:16】\nL113: \nL114: asked Apr 22, 2022 at 21:57\nL115: \nL116: 【24†a3nm】\nL117: \nL118: 592 3 3 silver badges 15 15 bronze badges\nL119:   * Seems like the \"straight polyominos\" (those obtained as a union of the squares touching a given segment, which need not be horizontal or vertical) should give the $\\Omega(n^2)$ lower bound, I'll write something tomorrow if no one has answered that part of the question\nL120: \nL121: –  【25†Saúl RM】\nL122: \nL123: Commented Apr 22, 2022 at 22:22\nL124: \nL125:   * You can at least get $S_n=\\Omega(n\\log n)$ by a simple argument based on the rectangles.\nL126: \nL127: –  【26†Steven Stadnicki】\nL128: \nL129: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 1:56\nL130: \nL131:   * 2\nL132: The case of the \"straight polyominos\" proposed by Saúl is the digital equivalent of the celebrated Kakeya problem: 【27†en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kakeya_set†en.wikipedia.org】\nL133: \nL134: –  【28†domotorp】\nL135: \nL136: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 5:13\nL137: \nL138:   * 2\nL139: @a3nm Take a nice set $S$ whose area is $<0.01n^2$ and contains a length $n$ segment in every direction. Such a set $S$ exists by the constructions given on Wikipeida for the Kakeya set. Now pixelize $S$ by checking which unit squares it intersects from the unit grid. The number of these squares is $0.01n^2 +O(n)$ as the perimeter of $S$ is nice. As $n\\to \\infty$, this gives an upper bound of $0.01n^2$, and we can pick $0.01$ as small as we want to.\nL140: \nL141: –  【28†domotorp】\nL142: \nL143: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 8:53\nL144: \nL145:   * 1\nL146: I've asked about something similar about free polyominoes on 【29†Code Golf Stack Exchange†codegolf.stackexchange.com】 and 【30†Math Stack Exchange†math.stackexchange.com】.\nL147: \nL148: –  【31†Peter Kagey】\nL149: \nL150: Commented Apr 24, 2022 at 2:11\nL151: \nL152:  |  【32†Show 17 more comments】\nL153: ## 3 Answers\nL154: \nL155: Sorted by:\nL156: \nL157: [Select]\nL158: \nL159: [Input]\nL160: \nL161: 9\nL162: \nL163: [Input]\nL164: It is actually $\\ge cn^2$ with some $c>0$. The value of $c$ I'll obtain is pretty dismal but I tried to trade the precision for the argument simplicity everywhere I could, so it can be certainly improved quite a bit. I have no doubt that it is written somewhere (perhaps, in the continuous form: the $2$-dimensional measure of a set containing a shift of every rectifiable curve of length $1$ is at least some positive constant) but I'll leave it to more educated people to provide the reference.\nL165: We shall work on the 2D $n\\times n$ lattice torus $T$ whose size $n$ is a power of $2$. Clearly, wrapping around makes the set only smaller. Define $K$ to be the integer such that $2^{K^3+K}\\le n< 2^{(K+1)^3+K+1}$ (I assume that $n$ is large enough, so $K$ is not too small either).\nL166: \nL167: Put $\\varepsilon_k=2^{-k}, M_k=2^{k^3}$ ($k\\ge 4$). Note that $\\frac 12+3\\sum_{k\\ge 4}\\varepsilon_k=\\frac 78<1$. Put $\\mu_k=\\frac 12+3\\sum_{m=4}^{k}\\varepsilon_m$ (so $\\mu_3=\\frac 12$).\nL168: Now take any set $E\\subset T$ of density $d(E,T)=\\frac{|E|}{|T|}=1/2$. Our aim will be to construct a connected set $P$ of cardinality $|P|\\le Cn$ such that no its lattice shift of $E$ on $T$ contains $P$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncode challenge - Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes - Code Golf Stack Exchange (https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/questions/167484/smallest-region-of-the-plane-that-contains-all-free-n-ominoes)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":140}); Total lines: 2664\nL45: # Example\nL46: \nL47: A nine-cell region is the smallest subset of the plane that can contain all twelve free 【20†5-ominoes†en.wikipedia.org】, as illustrated below. (A free polyomino is one that can be rotated and flipped.)\nL48: \nL49: (A twelve-cell region is the smallest subset of the plane the can contain all 35 free 【21†6-ominoes†en.wikipedia.org】.)\nL50: \nL51: * * *\nL52: # The Challenge\nL53: \nL54: Compute upper bounds on the smallest regions of the plane that can contain all n-ominoes as a function of n.\nL55: \nL56: Such a table begins:\nL57: \nL58:     `n | size\nL59:     --+-------\nL60:     1 | 1*\nL61:     2 | 2*\nL62:     3 | 4*\nL63:     4 | 6*\nL64:     5 | 9*\nL65:     6 | 12*\nL66:     7 | 37\nL67:     8 | 50\nL68:     9 | 65\nL69: \nL70:     *These values are the smallest possible.\nL71:     `\nL72: \nL73: * * *\nL74: # Example submission\nL75: \nL76:     `1-omino: 1\nL77:     #\nL78: \nL79:     2-omino: 2\nL80:     ##\nL81: \nL82:     3-omino: 4\nL83:     ###\nL84:      #\nL85: \nL86:     4-omino: 6\nL87:     ####\nL88:      ##\nL89: \nL90:     5-omino: 9\nL91:      #\nL92:     #####\nL93:      ###\nL94: \nL95:     6-omino: 12\nL96:     ####\nL97:     ######\nL98:       ##\nL99: \nL100:     7-omino: <= 37\nL101:     #######\nL102:     ######\nL103:     ######\nL104:     ######\nL105:     ######\nL106:     ######\nL107:     `\nL108: \nL109: * * *\nL110: # Scoring\nL111: \nL112: Run your program for as long as you'd like, and post your list of upper bounds together with the shapes that achieve each.\nL113: \nL114: The winner will be the participant whose table is lexicographically the earliest (with \"infinity\" appended to the shorter submissions.) If two participants submit the same results, then the earlier submission wins.\nL115: \nL116: For example, if the submissions are\nL117: \nL118:     `Aliyah: [1,2,4,6,12,30,50]\nL119:     Brian:  [1,2,4,6,12,37,49,65]\nL120:     Clare:  [1,2,4,6,12,30]\nL121:     `\nL122: then Aliyah wins. She beats Brian because 30 < 37, and she beats Clare because 50 < infinity.\nL123: \nL124:   * 【22†code-challenge】\nL125:   * 【23†sequence】\nL126:   * 【24†geometry】\nL127:   * 【25†combinatorics】\nL128:   * 【26†polyomino】\nL129: \nL130: 【27†Improve this question】\nL131: \nL132: Follow [Input]\nL133: \nL134: 【28†edited Jun 28, 2018 at 19:48】\nL135: \nL136: asked Jun 26, 2018 at 18:02\nL137: \nL138: 【29†Peter Kagey】\nL139: \nL140: 8,385 1 1 gold badge 29 29 silver badges 122 122 bronze badges\nL141: \nL142:   * 2\nL143: \nL144: Interesting challenge, I will work on this later today!\nL145: \nL146: –  【30†orlp】\nL147: \nL148: Commented Jun 26, 2018 at 18:23\nL149: \nL150:   * 1\nL151: Polyominos with or without holes?\nL152: \nL153: –  【31†Peter Taylor】\nL154: \nL155: Commented Jun 27, 2018 at 6:43\nL156: \nL157:   * Do programs have to run deterministically?\nL158: \nL159: –  【32†N. Virgo】\nL160: \nL161: Commented Jun 27, 2018 at 14:09\nL162: \nL163:   * 1\nL164: \nL165: @PeterTaylor, polyominoes with holes—but my naïve expectation is that it wouldn't change the result.\nL166: \nL167: –  【29†Peter Kagey】\nL168: \nL169: Commented Jun 27, 2018 at 16:06\nL170: \nL171:   * 1\nL172: Technically, I think you're only interested in lattice aligned placements of the polyominoes, right? You can't rotate the piece by 23.42132 degrees, right?\nL173: \nL174: –  【33†izzyg】\nL175: \nL176: Commented Jun 29, 2018 at 6:13\nL177: \nL178:  |  【34†Show 2 more comments】\nL179: ## 4 Answers\nL180: \nL181: Sorted by:\nL182: \nL183: [Select]\nL184: \nL185: [Input]\nL186: \nL187: 11\nL188: \nL189: [Input]\nL190: \nL191: +150\nL192: ## C# and SAT: 1, 2, 4, 6, 9, 12, 17, 20, 26, 31, 37, 43\nL193: \nL194: If we limit the bounding box, there is a fairly obvious expression of the problem in terms of 【35†SAT†en.wikipedia.org】: each translation of each orientation of each free polyomino is a large conjunction; for each polyomino we form a disjunction over its conjunctions; and then we require each disjunction to be true and the total number of cells used to be limited.\nL195: To limit the number of cells my initial version built a full adder; then I used bitonic sort for unary counting (similar to 【36†this earlier answer】 but generalised); finally I settled on the approach described by Bailleux and Boufkhad in 【37†Efficient CNF encoding of Boolean cardinality constraints†citeseerx.ist.psu.edu】.\nL196: I wanted to make the post self-contained, so I dug out a C# implementation of a SAT solver with a BSD licence which was state-of-the-art about 15 years ago, replaced its NIH list implementation with `System.Collections.Generic.List<T>` (gaining a factor of 2 in speed), golfed it from 50kB down to 31kB to fit in the 64kB post limit, and then did some aggressive work on reducing memory usage. This code can obviously be adapted to output a DIMACS file which can then be passed to more modern solvers.\nL197: ## Solutions found\nL198: \nL199:     `#\nL200: \nL201:     ##\nL202: \nL203:     ###\nL204:     ..#\nL205: \nL206:     ####\nL207:     .##.\nL208: \nL209:     ..#..\nL210:     #####\nL211:     ..###\nL212: \nL213:     .####.\nL214:     ######\nL215:     .##...\nL216: \nL217:     ....#..\nL218:     #######\nL219:     #####..\nL220:     .####..\nL221: \nL222:     ########\nL223:     ..######\nL224:     .....###\nL225:     .....###\nL226: \nL227:     #########\nL228:     #######..\nL229:     ..#####..\nL230:     ....##...\nL231:     ....###..\nL232: \nL233:     ##########\nL234:     ########..\nL235:     ..######..\nL236:     ....####..\nL237:     .....###..\nL238: \nL239:     ..#######..\nL240:     ..#########\nL241:     ###########\nL242:     ..####.....\nL243:     ..####.....\nL244:     ..##.......\nL245: \nL246:     ...#######..\nL247:     ...#########\nL248:     ############\nL249:     ..#####....#\nL250:     ..#####.....\nL251:     ...####.....\nL252:     `\nL253: To find 43 for n=12 took a bit over 7.5 hours.\nL254: ### Polyomino code\nL255: \nL256:     `using MiniSAT;\nL257:     using System;\nL258:     using System.Collections.Generic;\nL259:     using System.Diagnostics;\nL260:     using System.Linq;\nL261: \nL262:     namespace PPCG167484\nL263:     {\nL264:         internal class SatGenerator\nL265:         {\nL266:             public static void Main()\nL267:             {\nL268:                 for (int n = 1; n < 13; n++)\nL269:                 {\nL270:                     int width = n;\nL271:                     int height = (n + 1) >> 1;\nL272:                     var polys = FreePolyomino.All(n);\nL273:                     (var solver, var unaryWeights) = Generate(polys, width, height);\nL274: \nL275:                     int previous = width * height + 1;\nL276:                     while (true)\nL277:                     {\nL278:                         Stopwatch sw = new Stopwatch(); sw.Start();\nL279:                         if (solver.Solve())\nL280:                         {\nL281:                             // The weight of the solution might be smaller than the target\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.csun.edu/~ctoth/Handbook/chap14.pdf)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"lineno\":85}); Total lines: 1139\nL34@P0-1: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.360 G. Barequet, S.W. Golomb, and D.A. Klarner\nL35@P1: 14.2 EQUIVALENCE OF POLYOMINOES\nL36@P1: Notions of equivalence for polyominoes are defined in terms of groups of affine maps\nL37@P1: that act on the set Z\nL38@P1: 2\nL39@P1: of cells in the plane.\nL40@P1: GLOSSARY\nL41@P1: Translation by (r, s): The mapping from Z\nL42@P1: 2\nL43@P1: to itself that maps [u, v] to [u +\nL44@P1: r, v + s]; it sends any subset S ⊂ Z\nL45@P1: 2\nL46@P1: to its translate S + (r, s) = {[u + r, v + s] :\nL47@P1: [u, v] ∈ S}.\nL48@P1: Translation-equivalent: Sets S, S′ of cells such that S\nL49@P1: ′\nL50@P1: is a translate of S.\nL51@P1: Fixed polyomino: A translation-equivalence class of polyominoes; t(n) denotes\nL52@P1: the number of fixed n-ominoes. ((A(n) is also widely used in the literature.)\nL53@P1: Representatives of the six fixed 3-ominoes are shown in Figure 14.2.1.\nL54@P1: FIGURE 14.2.1\nL55@P1: The six fixed 3-ominoes.\nL56@P1: The lexicographic cell ordering ≺ on Z\nL57@P1: 2\nL58@P1: is defined by: [r, s] ≺ [u, v] if s < v, or\nL59@P1: if s = v and r < u.\nL60@P1: Standard position: The translate S −(u, v) of S, where [u, v] is the lexicograph\u0002ically minimum cell in S.\nL61@P1: A finite set S ⊂ Z\nL62@P1: 2\nL63@P1: is in standard position if and only if [0, 0] ∈ S, v ≥ 0 for all\nL64@P1: [u, v] ∈ S, and u ≥ 0 for all [u, 0] ∈ S.\nL65@P1: Rotation-translation group: The group R of mappings of Z\nL66@P1: 2\nL67@P1: to itself of the\nL68@P1: form [u, v] 7→ [u, v]\nL69@P1: \u0014\nL70@P1: 0 −1\nL71@P1: 1 0 \u0015k\nL72@P1: + (r, s). (The matrix \u0014\nL73@P1: 0 −1\nL74@P1: 1 0 \u0015\nL75@P1: , which is de\u0002noted by R, maps [u, v] to [v, −u] by right multiplication, hence represents a\nL76@P1: clockwise rotation of 90◦.)\nL77@P1: Rotationally equivalent: Sets S, S′ of cells with S\nL78@P1: ′ = ρS for some ρ ∈ R.\nL79@P1: Chiral polyomino, or handed polyomino: A rotational-equivalence class of\nL80@P1: polyominoes; r(n) denotes the number of chiral n-ominoes.\nL81@P1: The top row of 5-ominoes in Figure 14.2.2 consists of the set of cells F = {[0, −1],\nL82@P1: [−1, 0], [0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1]}, together with F R, F R2, and F R3. All four of these\nL83@P1: 5-ominoes are rotationally equivalent. The bottom row in Figure 14.2.2 shows\nL84@P1: these same four 5-ominoes reflected about the x-axis. These four 5-ominoes are\nL85@P1: rotationally equivalent as well, but none of them is rotationally equivalent to\nL86@P1: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL87@P1-2: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.Chapter 14: Polyominoes 361\nL88@P2: any of the 5-ominoes shown in the top row. Representatives of the seven chiral\nL89@P2: 4-ominoes are shown in Figure 14.2.3.\nL90@P2: FIGURE 14.2.2\nL91@P2: The 5-ominoes in the top row are\nL92@P2: rotationally equivalent, and so are their\nL93@P2: reflections in the bottom row, but the\nL94@P2: two sets are rotationally distinct. FM FRM FR2M FR3M\nL95@P2: F FR FR FR 2 3\nL96@P2: FIGURE 14.2.3\nL97@P2: The seven chiral 4-ominoes.\nL98@P2: Congruence group:\nL99@P2: \u0014\nL100@P2: The group S of motions generated by the matrix M =\nL101@P2: 1 0\nL102@P2: 0 −1\nL103@P2: \u0015\nL104@P2: (reflection in the x-axis) and the rotation-translation group R. (A\nL105@P2: typical element of S has the form [u, v] 7→ [u, v]RkMi+(r, s), for some k = 0, 1, 2,\nL106@P2: or 3, some i = 0 or 1, and some r, s ∈ Z.)\nL107@P2: Congruent: Sets S and S\nL108@P2: ′ of cells such that S′ = σ(S) for some σ ∈ S.\nL109@P2: Free polyomino: A congruence class of polyominoes; s(n) denotes the number\nL110@P2: of free n-ominoes. The twelve free 5-ominoes are shown in Figure 14.2.4.\nL111@P2: THEOREM 14.2.1 Embedding Theorem\nL112@P2: For each n, let Un consist of the n\nL113@P2: 2 − n + 1 cells of the form [u, v], where\nL114@P2: \u001a\nL115@P2: 0 ≤ u ≤ n, for v = 0\nL116@P2: |u| + v ≤ n, for v > 0\nL117@P2: . (See Figure 14.2.5 for the case n = 5.) Then, all\nL118@P2: n-ominoes in standard position are edge-connected subsets of Un that contain [0, 0].\nL119@P2: COROLLARY 14.2.2\nL120@P2: The number of fixed n-ominoes is finite for each n.\nL121@P2: The same result can be obtained by a simple argument due to Eden [Ede61]:\nL122@P2: Every polyomino P of size n can be built according to a set of n−1 “instructions”\nL123@P2: taken from a superset of size 3(n−1). Starting with a single square, each instruction\nL124@P2: tells us how to choose a lattice cell c, neighboring a cell already in P, and add c to P.\nL125@P2: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL126@P2-3: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.362 G. Barequet, S.W. Golomb, and D.A. Klarner\nL127@P3: FIGURE 14.2.4\nL128@P3: The twelve free 5-ominoes.\nL129@P3: FIGURE 14.2.5\nL130@P3: A set of n\nL131@P3: 2 −n+ 1 cells (for n = 5) that contains\nL132@P3: every n-omino in standard position.\nL133@P3: x\nL134@P3: y\nL135@P3: (Some of these instruction sets are illegal, and some other sets produce the same\nL136@P3: polyominoes.) Hence, the number of polyominoes of size n is less than\nL137@P3: 3(n−1)\nL138@P3: n−1\nL139@P3: \u0001\nL140@P3: .\nL141@P3: 14.3 HOW MANY n-OMINOES ARE THERE?\nL142@P3: Table 14.3.1, calculated by Redelmeier [Red81], indicates the values of t(n), r(n),\nL143@P3: and s(n) for n = 1, . . . , 24. Jensen and Guttmann [JG00, Jen01] and Jensen [Jen03]\nL144@P3: extended the enumeration of polyominoes up to n = 56. See also sequence A001168\nL145@P3: in the OEIS (On-line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences) [oeis].\nL146@P3: It is easy to see that for each n, we have\nL147@P3: t(n)\nL148@P3: 8\nL149@P3: ≤ s(n) ≤ r(n) ≤ t(n).\nL150@P3: The values of t(n) seem to be growing exponentially, and indeed they have expo\u0002nential bounds.\nL151@P3: THEOREM 14.3.1 [Kla67]\nL152@P3: limn→∞(t(n))1/n = λ exists.\nL153@P3: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL154@P3-4: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.Chapter 14: Polyominoes 363\nL155@P4: TABLE 14.3.1 The number of fixed, chiral, and\nL156@P4: free n-ominoes for n ≤ 24.\nL157@P4: n t(n) r(n) s(n)\nL158@P4: 1 1 1 1\nL159@P4: 2 2 1 1\nL160@P4: 3 6 2 2\nL161@P4: 4 19 7 5\nL162@P4: 5 63 18 12\nL163@P4: 6 216 60 35\nL164@P4: 7 760 196 108\nL165@P4: 8 2725 704 369\nL166@P4: 9 9910 2500 1285\nL167@P4: 10 36446 9189 4655\nL168@P4: 11 135268 33896 17073\nL169@P4: 12 505861 126759 63600\nL170@P4: 13 1903890 476270 238591\nL171@P4: 14 7204874 1802312 901971\nL172@P4: 15 27394666 6849777 3426576\nL173@P4: 16 104592937 26152418 13079255\nL174@P4: 17 400795844 100203194 50107909\nL175@P4: 18 1540820542 385221143 192622052\nL176@P4: 19 5940738676 1485200848 742624232\nL177@P4: 20 22964779660 5741256764 28706